  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers tonight into Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure will exit across the Maritimes tonight, while a \ncold front crosses the region and an upper trof approaches from\nthe west. The heavier rains with the surface low will remain\noffshore across the Gulf of Maine. However, the northern edge \nof the precipitation shield will bring rain to Downeast areas \nearly tonight. An isolated thunderstorm is also possible along \nthe Downeast coast early tonight. Precipitation then tapers to \nshowers Downeast overnight. Across northern portions of the \nforecast area, showers will accompany the cold front. An \nisolated thunderstorm is also possible early tonight. Shower \nchances will then diminish in the wake of the cold front. The \nupper trof will cross the region early Monday with \nisolated/scattered morning showers. Rainfall with this system \nwill have minimal impact on the drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy/areas of frost possible across\nnorthern areas Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region Monday night,  \nbringing clear skies and light winds. This will result in \nfavorable conditions for radiational cooling. Low temperatures \nwill generally range from the mid to upper 30s across much of \nnorthern Maine. However, temperatures could locally drop to \naround freezing across northwest areas. Patchy/areas of frost \nare possible Monday night across northern areas, particularly \nin the normally colder low lying locations. Patchy river valley\nfog is also possible later Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance for rain showers by late next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system to the far north moves eastward, pushing a\nfront through New England late next week. Models disagree on \nthe timing of the pressure system, with some models suggesting\nthe system will move through by the end of the week while others\ndelay into the weekend. Not anticipating enough rainfall to \nprovide drought improvement at this time.",
